messages:
anya: Heads-up — ran into a nasty edge on Android where Retrofit + OkHttp will replay streaming request bodies during a retry/401 refresh and cause duplicate uploads. Repro with file upload + token refresh flow. :facepalm:
kevin: ugh. which OkHttp setting? retryOnConnectionFailure? interceptor ordering?
anya: We had retryOnConnectionFailure=true (default), plus an authenticator that does a synchronous refresh on 401. If the original request body is a streaming/one-shot body (e.g., from InputStreamSource), call.clone() ends up replaying a request with an empty body or re-sending same stream and the server treats it as a duplicate. Stack traces show 401 -> authenticator -> client.newCall(original).execute() -> body write fails sometimes.
sam: can you paste the authenticator snippet?
anya: Sure:
```java
Authenticator auth = (route, response) -> {
  synchronized (tokenLock) {
    if (isRefreshing) return null;
    isRefreshing = true;
    try {
      String newTok = refresher.refresh(); // blocking call to refresh token
      return response.request().newBuilder()
        .header("Authorization", "Bearer " + newTok)
        .build();
    } finally { isRefreshing = false; }
  }
};
```
anya: The bug is twofold: 1) non-repeatable RequestBody (stream) can't be re-sent, 2) OkHttp will attempt retries / connection recovery and then our authenticator triggers an additional call path that duplicates payloads.
kevin: Are we hitting this on specific Android API levels?
anya: Mostly on older Android devices (API 21-25) with limited memory where we stream large attachments instead of buffering them. Also observed when TLS renegotiation or connection reset occurs, OkHttp retries under the hood.
sam: short-term mitigation: disable automatic connection retries and make the body repeatable when possible. e.g. wrap the body in a buffered byte[] or temp file. Sample helper:
```java
static RequestBody makeRepeatable(RequestBody original) throws IOException {
  Buffer b = new Buffer();
  original.writeTo(b);
  byte[] bytes = b.readByteArray();
  return RequestBody.create(original.contentType(), bytes);
}
```
sam: downside: memory + perf. For large attachments use a File-backed RequestBody.
leo: Another option is to make the authenticator return null and surface 401 to caller, then let caller retry with a fresh request built from a repeatable body. Might be nicer for mobile UX.
kevin: +1 to surface pattern in samples. This is something we should document in SDK docs: "authenticator best practices with streaming bodies" and add a test in sample app that simulates connection reset.
anya: Agreed. I'll add a small sample to the android-samples repo showing a FileRequestBody and toggles: retryOnConnectionFailure(false) vs true. Also will add a note about OkHttp call.clone() not making non-repeatable bodies repeatable.
ci-bot: Created issue #3421: "Android SDK: avoid duplicate uploads when using streaming RequestBody + authenticator" — https://github.com/redwood-inference/android-samples/issues/3421 :robot_face:
leo: Nice. Can we also add a lint warning in the SDK when a non-repeatable body is used with an authenticator? At least log.warn.
sam: Yeah, we can detect if the RequestBody throws on writeTo twice and log a warning.
anya: I'll draft the PR for the sample + docs. Tagging @kevin @sam for review. Thanks everyone :thumbsup:
